  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nCurrent-Saturday...Very muggy conditions exist with temps well into \nthe 70s. Surface high pressure ridging north of the area today will \nslowly slip south toward central FL into Sat. Light morning S/SW \nwinds will transition onshore into the afternoon with ECSB formation \nand slow push inland. Should expect a late day/early evening sea \nbreeze collision across the interior later today, but the collision \nwill be closer towards the east coast on Sat. Storm steering is \nlight SWRLY today and more WRLY on Sat. Weak troughing in the mid-\nlevels will exist along the FL east coast with some mid-level energy \navailable for aiding convection, though temps at 500 mb remain \nfairly warm at -5.0C to -5.5C. PWATs remain in excess of 2\" and \ndiurnal PoPs 50-70% (highest interior) today and Sat. Primary \nstorm impacts remain frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds \nlocally 35-45 mph, and torrential downpours capable of 2-4\" in a \nrelatively short period promoting minor/nuisance flooding in slow-\nmoving storms. The Weather Prediction Center maintains a Marginal\n(level 1 of 4) Risk for excessive rainfall through at least \nSaturday. \n\nMax temps near to slightly above climo near 90F to L90s. Humid \novernight with mins in the 70s. Peak heat indices will reach 100-\n107F ahead of daily storms.\n\nSun-Thu...Troughiness in the mid-levels encompasses the eastern \nseaboard and western Atlc thru early week before finally pushing \nseaward into mid-week. Mid-level ridging will move out of the \nMountain West/Great Plains and into the Deep South to include the FL \npeninsula by mid-week. Temperatures aloft remain fairly warm. At the \nsurface, a weak pressure pattern will remain in place. A weakening \nfront will push into the Deep South late Sun/early next week. This \nwill act to draw deep moisture into the area into at least Mon, but \nthere will be some drier air intrusion ahead of mid-week. Continue \nhigher PoPs with 70% areawide on Sun, 80% areawide Mon, 60-70% \nTue, with perhaps a bit more of a gradual drying trend into \nmid/late week. Storm motion still light, but more offshore-moving \ninto Tue. Generally near seasonal temps for highs (U80s-L90s) with\nlows in the 70s continuing.",
